      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> MOU voided July 18, US troops killed in Jordan July 17, IRGC strikes expanding to GCC energy infrastructure, and 8 consecutive nights of US strikes on Iran define the active structural escalation dynamic; <span class="cite">F1</span> (Fordow/Arak strike, p=0.20 over 72h) is the near-term trigger indicator, with systemic multi-theater expansion risk running materially above that single-event probability.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Neither side crosses nuclear-facility or WMD threshold, IRGC attrition against GCC and US assets continues at current tempo, Hormuz remains near-closed through the <span class="cite">F3</span> horizon (complement 0.72), mediation channels degraded but not destroyed, conflict settles into sustained low-intensity exchange with intermittent strike sequences.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> A named mediator publicly confirms ceasefire-proposal relay to both sides (<span class="cite">F2</span>, p=0.28), Hormuz transit volume recovers above a sustained 7-day average of 20/day (<span class="cite">F3</span>, p=0.28), with joint probability approximately 0.22 given the structural correlation between <span class="cite">F2</span> and <span class="cite">F3</span> outcomes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
